Migrations
==========

Run with `threadsift db-upgrade` (or `alembic upgrade head`). Rolling back to
`base` (`alembic downgrade base`) drops all data.

Review rule: never touch the `author_type` type after the first migration
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

`author_type` is a Postgres enum that the first migration creates EMPTY. The
operator fills it with their own values (`ALTER TYPE author_type ADD VALUE
'...'`, see the main README). Those values live only in the operator's database,
not in the code.

So every migration after 0001 MUST NOT:

  - drop, recreate or rename `author_type`,
  - run `ALTER TYPE author_type ...`,
  - change the type of `authors.type`,
  - add or remove enum values on the operator's behalf.

If a change seems to need any of that, stop and ask: it would silently destroy
or invalidate the operator's list of values on upgrade. Reject such migrations
in review.

Views and triggers (added after 0001) depend on `authors.type`. They are kept
in their own migrations so the operator can drop them, recreate the type to
remove a value, and bring them back (see "Removing a value" in the main README).
